  "scenario": "A buyer wants one imaging path for detailed daylight subjects, dim indoor scenes, distant subjects and an occasional large print. Six frozen seller pages look impressive but use unlike claims. TCL lists a 249.99-dollar phone with a 64MP main camera and AI-powered quad-camera wording. DJI lists a 499-dollar Pocket 2 with a 1/1.7-inch sensor, 64MP photos, 8x zoom and an AI Editor that combines clips. Nikon lists a 6.24-dollar B500 bundle rated 73 percent over twelve reviews with a 16MP sensor, 40x optical zoom, 80x enhanced digital zoom and a 64GB memory card. VJIANGER lists a 119.99-dollar camera rated 72 percent over ten reviews with 48MP and 16x digital-zoom claims. Panasonic lists a 997.99-dollar body-only G9 with a 20.3MP sensor and an 80MP RAW special mode created from eight shots. KUIDAMOS lists a 73.69-dollar child camera with no posted review, two lenses described as 2400W (720P), 200 DPI thermal printing and AI wording for autofocus, color or discoloration and dimming. General pages distinguish pixel count, image resolution, resolved optical detail, optical zoom, digital crop enlargement, binning, Bayer sampling, demosaicing, computational photography and super-resolution. Community pages show how people colloquially equate megapixels with resolution, discuss optical zoom on phones and report an image-quality complaint confounded by a lens protector. None supplies a matched test of the six exact offers or validates every ambiguous unit and AI label.",
